How do you make a narrow London living room feel wider? In many London homes, particularly Victorian and Edwardian properties, living rooms can be long and relatively narrow. That doesn't mean they have to feel cramped. Through tailored <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/residential-interior-design-london/">luxury residential interior design London</a> homeowners can unlock spaciousness using the right layout, furniture proportions, lighting and colour choices.

The key is to work with the proportions of the room rather than trying to fill every available space.
<h2>What Layout Changes Can Make a Narrow Living Room Feel Wider?</h2>
<img class="wp-image-6914 size-full" src="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Untitled-design-15.jpg" alt="Cozy living room with a lit fireplace, two beige armchairs, a sofa, patterned rug, decorative plants, and framed artwork above the mantle." width="1920" height="1080" />
<h3>Should furniture run along the longest wall?</h3>
It can be tempting to push every piece of furniture against the walls, but this isn't always the best solution. Leaving some breathing space around furniture can actually make the room feel less restricted.

A sofa positioned along the longer wall can work well, particularly when paired with a smaller coffee table and carefully chosen occasional chairs. Avoid using furniture that is too deep for the room, as this can quickly reduce the available walkway. To see how these proportion principles apply across modern residences, explore our guide on <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/how-to-design-a-contemporary-home/">how to design a contemporary home</a>.

For more guidance, these <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/how-to-design-your-living-room-layout-a-complete-guide/"><strong>living room layout</strong></a> ideas can help you think about proportions and circulation.
<h3>Can you divide a long room into different zones?</h3>
Yes, and doing so can make a narrow room feel more purposeful.

Instead of arranging everything in one straight line, consider creating a seating area at one end and a reading, dining or workspace area at the other, depending on the size of the room.

A rug, pendant or change in furniture arrangement can subtly define each area without physically dividing the room, drawing inspiration from minimalist <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/scandinavian-living-room-ideas-create-a-calm-and-inviting-space/">Scandinavian living room ideas</a> to maintain visual lightness.
<h3>What furniture works best in a narrow room?</h3>
Scale is particularly important.

Choose furniture that gives you enough comfort without overwhelming the available floor space. Slimmer occasional chairs, smaller side tables and furniture with visible legs can help maintain a sense of openness.

Avoid filling the room with too many individual pieces. A smaller number of well-proportioned items will often make the space feel calmer, a spatial discipline honed by our team across prime homes as well as commercial environments when <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/designing-retail-spaces-that-captivate-a-guide-to-stunning-interiors/">designing retail spaces that captivate</a>.
<h2>How Can Colour, Lighting and Storage Change the Sense of Space?</h2>
<img class="alignnone wp-image-7123 size-full" src="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sfwe.jpg" alt="Cozy living room with olive‑green walls, white brick fireplace, brown leather sofa, wooden coffee table, patterned rug and large windows." width="867" height="575" />
<h3>Can colour make a narrow living room appear wider?</h3>
Colour can influence how the proportions of a room are perceived.

Lighter tones can help reflect natural light and create an airy feeling, while using similar shades across walls, ceilings and larger furniture can reduce visual breaks.

That doesn't mean the room needs to be completely white. Warm neutrals, soft greens, muted blues and other gentle tones can create depth without making the room feel enclosed. If you love richer, tranquil accent tones, consider the palette techniques highlighted in our <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/teal-bedroom-decor-ideas-elegant-calming-timeless/">teal bedroom decor ideas</a> guide.
<h3>How can lighting help?</h3>
Good lighting can make a narrow room feel much more balanced.

Rather than relying on a single ceiling light, use several sources at different levels. Wall lights, table lamps and floor lamps can draw attention to different parts of the room and prevent the space from feeling dark at one end, which forms a vital part of <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/interior-architecture/">prime London interior architecture</a>.

Natural light is equally important. These <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/effect-of-natural-lighting-in-interiors/"><strong>natural lighting</strong></a> principles can help you understand how daylight influences the way a room feels.
<h3>Can built-in storage make the room feel larger?</h3>
Well-planned storage can actually make a narrow living room feel more spacious by reducing visual clutter.

Instead of several freestanding cabinets, consider using one continuous piece of cabinetry along part of a wall. It can provide storage for books, media equipment and everyday items while creating a cleaner visual line, as demonstrated in our <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/knightsbridge-residence-interior-design/">Knightsbridge luxury apartment case study</a>.

For smaller properties, thinking about <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/interior-design-for-small-spaces-stylish-solutions-that-maximise-every-inch/"><strong>small spaces</strong></a> as a whole can also help you find solutions that improve both storage and circulation. When structural alterations are required to open up layouts, evaluating <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/design-and-build-vs-separate-contractors-luxury-renovation/">design and build vs separate contractors in luxury renovation</a> will ensure a streamlined delivery process.

Ultimately, making a narrow London living room feel wider isn't about making everything smaller. It's about choosing the right proportions and giving the room enough visual and physical breathing space.

